    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d70">

                <pb facs="0181" n="153" sameAs="frus:frus1955-57v26/pg153"/>

                <head>70. Memorandum of a Conversation Between the Secretary of State and the
                    Ambassador to Germany (Conant), Department of State,
                    Washington, September 6, 1956, 12:45 p.m.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d70-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            type="conversation-or-meeting-date" when="1956-09-06T12:45:00-04:00"><hi
                                rendition="#italic">September 6, 1956, 12:45
                        p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>We discussed the status of Adenauer and his
                    viewpoint and apparent misunderstanding of many of the present problems. I
                    reported to Ambassador Conant the talks which I had had with Foreign Minister
                    Von Brentano and with Dr. Krone.<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d70-f2"/> Ambassador Conant said that obviously Adenauer was getting a lot of misinformation and
                    that he, Conant, was quite shocked by Adenauer’s letter to me.</p>
                <p>Ambassador Conant then referred to the suggestion that he should go as Ambassador
                    to India and expressed again his regret that he had felt compelled to indicate
                    his unavailability. He said he was <pb facs="0182" n="154" xml:id="pg154"/>
                    planning to get back here for work with foundations, etc., around the first of
                    the year and asked about what would be desired in the timing of his resignation.
                    I said I thought he should follow the usual procedure and put in his resignation
                    after the November election. It would then be acted upon in due course with a
                    view probably to an actual change early in January. Ambassador Conant said that
                    that would fit in very well with his personal plans.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold"
                    >JFD</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d70-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d70-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Eisenhower Library, Dulles Papers,
                    General Memoranda of Conversation. Secret; Personal and Private. Drafted by
                        Dulles. On August 4, Dulles had
                    telephoned Conant to ask whether he would accept an appointment as Ambassador to
                    India. Conant had been surprised and “his first reaction was strongly negative,”
                    but he said he would consider the matter further and discuss it again when he
                    returned to Washington. (<hi rendition="#italic">Ibid.</hi>)</p>
            </note>
